{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790471554,"nonce":"Yvzq5bIeril81ujdoOevwJNKPEg=","message":"Still open on e64fde52. NUC, 10 interleaved rounds, efedddf8 (before 80749056) against e64fde52: bench_op_overload_ref wall 43.65 ms (41.6 to 59.4) against 51.33 (46.5 to 53.9), 1.18x; bench_op_overload 93.67 (88.5 to 102.5) against 98.04 (93.6 to 105.1), overlapping. The typed site already calls the trusted instance (Vec3$__add__$s0$trusted); what costs is what the build keeps.\n\nWhere the reach comes from: zb_hook_instance_arith, which the frontend generates for every class defining an operator dunder, names the item's own function (Vec3$__add__, other: Object). The hook is built at the start of lowering (the first owed round already lists Vec3$__add__ beside the exception constructors the other hooks name), so the generic is lowered, and every own function lowered is an entry name for the prune; its body reaches py$getattr$x/y/z, zb_any_arith, and through zb_any_arith the dynamic eq/hash and the set, dict and list families: the kept set is 215 functions against 117 for a program with no class. Before 80749056 the generic's `other` was typed Vec3 and reached nothing of that.\n\nTwo ways out, neither frontend-local: (a) lowering builds a program-defined hook only once a library body calling it (zb_any_arith here) is reached, and the prune roots only what is reached from the entry rather than every own function lowered (crates/compiler lowering.rs defer_own_functions / crates/zyntax_embed tiered.rs entered names); or (b) the frontend leaves out of the hook every class whose instances are never boxed, which needs every boxing path recorded (coerce Class to Object, list/dict/set/tuple conversions of class elements, shaped and keyed hooks, dynamic-call and getattr adapters), with the arith hook built last; a path missed turns a working dynamic `a + b` into a TypeError, so (a) is the safer fix.","files":null}]}